WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.373
Begin by framing Course Purpose and Scope as a
question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:08.373 --> 00:00:09.562
observable results.

3
00:00:09.562 --> 00:00:17.343
A research project narrows a question, reviews
literature, defines a reproducible method,

4
00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:22.531
evaluates results, and communicates for the
intended audience.

5
00:00:22.531 --> 00:00:29.246
Consider this worked case: A student might
develop a novel algorithm for data compression

6
00:00:29.246 --> 00:00:33.562
or design a prototype for a student-led tech
startup.

7
00:00:33.562 --> 00:00:40.751
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:40.751 --> 00:00:41.265
conclusion.

9
00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:47.531
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:47.642 --> 00:00:56.344
Now define Proposal Drafting Process precisely
enough that another learner could test the

11
00:00:56.344 --> 00:00:57.013
definition.

12
00:00:57.013 --> 00:01:02.865
A student works with a potential faculty
sponsor to draft a proposal that describes

13
00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:09.552
what a student plans to do, what deliverables
are expected, how the student's work will be

14
00:01:09.552 --> 00:01:14.986
evaluated, and how much credit will be
assigned for successful completion of the

15
00:01:14.986 --> 00:01:15.404
work.

16
00:01:15.404 --> 00:01:22.424
Consider this worked case: The proposal
specifies a three-month timeline, a working

17
00:01:22.424 --> 00:01:30.029
prototype as the deliverable, peer review as
evaluation, and 3 credits for completion.

18
00:01:30.029 --> 00:01:36.388
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

19
00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:38.701
evidence would distinguish them.

20
00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:45.263
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

21
00:01:45.362 --> 00:01:53.132
With the foundation in place, examine how
Faculty Sponsor Role operates in a concrete

22
00:01:53.132 --> 00:01:53.687
sequence.

23
00:01:53.687 --> 00:02:00.030
Students work with a supervising faculty
member on topics of mutual interest.

24
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:06.646
Consider this worked case: The sponsor
provides technical feedback on the methodology

25
00:02:06.646 --> 00:02:11.608
and confirms that the project aligns with
departmental resources.

26
00:02:11.608 --> 00:02:18.952
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

27
00:02:18.952 --> 00:02:24.686
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

28
00:02:24.686 --> 00:02:32.102
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

29
00:02:32.102 --> 00:02:34.327
throughout the analysis.

30
00:02:34.442 --> 00:02:41.318
Next, compare the choices involved in Grade
Proposal Procedure and make the decision

31
00:02:41.318 --> 00:02:42.376
criteria explicit.

32
00:02:42.376 --> 00:02:49.611
The faculty sponsor proposes the grade, but
before the grade is officially recorded, the

33
00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:55.813
student must submit a final report that
summarizes what was actually accomplished.

34
00:02:55.813 --> 00:03:02.806
Consider this worked case: The sponsor
recommends a B+ after reviewing the final

35
00:03:02.806 --> 00:03:09.798
deliverables, but the grade remains pending
until the student submits the final report.

36
00:03:09.798 --> 00:03:16.775
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

37
00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:17.938
the goalposts.

38
00:03:17.938 --> 00:03:24.845
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

39
00:03:24.962 --> 00:03:32.410
Apply Final Report Requirement to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

40
00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:33.474
isolated definition.

41
00:03:33.474 --> 00:03:35.787
They should respect privacy.

42
00:03:35.787 --> 00:03:43.210
Consider this worked case: The report includes
a summary of tasks completed, challenges

43
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:48.349
encountered, and evidence of deliverables
meeting the proposed criteria.

44
00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:55.805
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:55.805 --> 00:03:56.302
step.

46
00:03:56.302 --> 00:04:01.302
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:04:01.302 --> 00:04:08.165
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

48
00:04:08.165 --> 00:04:10.224
throughout the analysis.

49
00:04:10.346 --> 00:04:15.706
Examine the tradeoffs in Departmental
Permission Requirement, including what

50
00:04:15.706 --> 00:04:19.280
improves and what becomes more difficult.

51
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.389
Enrollment in this course requires permission
from the department offering the course.

52
00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:32.754
Consider this worked case: The student submits
a formal request to the CS department chair,

53
00:04:32.754 --> 00:04:38.155
who approves the enrollment based on academic
standing and project feasibility.

54
00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:45.061
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

55
00:04:45.061 --> 00:04:52.764
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

56
00:04:52.874 --> 00:04:59.538
Stress-test the reasoning around Ethical
Standards in Independent Study with an edge

57
00:04:59.538 --> 00:05:03.980
case and identify which assumption controls
the outcome.

58
00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:07.214
Computing professionals should avoid harm.

59
00:05:07.214 --> 00:05:15.447
Consider this worked case: When developing a
student tracking app, the student ensures data

60
00:05:15.447 --> 00:05:20.152
is anonymized and obtains informed consent
from participants.

61
00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:26.792
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

62
00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:34.027
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

63
00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:41.721
Use Research Integrity and Communication to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

64
00:05:41.721 --> 00:05:44.251
causes, and corrective action.

65
00:05:44.251 --> 00:05:50.330
Responsible technical communication
distinguishes claims from evidence.

66
00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:58.128
Consider this worked case: The student's final
report cites three relevant papers, reports

67
00:05:58.128 --> 00:06:04.126
test results without selective editing, and
lists data collection constraints.

68
00:06:04.126 --> 00:06:11.667
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:13.283
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:06:13.283 --> 00:06:21.267
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:06:21.267 --> 00:06:28.406
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

72
00:06:28.406 --> 00:06:30.548
throughout the analysis.

73
00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:37.017
Connect Defining Verifiable Project
Deliverables to the earlier ideas and explain

74
00:06:37.017 --> 00:06:41.068
how the pieces work as one system.

75
00:06:41.068 --> 00:06:48.193
The proposal describes what the student plans
to do, the expected deliverables, how the work

76
00:06:48.193 --> 00:06:52.943
will be evaluated, and the credit assigned for
successful completion.

77
00:06:52.943 --> 00:07:00.636
Consider this worked case: A systems project
names a working prototype, a reproducible

78
00:07:00.636 --> 00:07:07.146
evaluation with stated measures, and a final
report as separate deliverables.

79
00:07:07.146 --> 00:07:14.185
Explain how each deliverable supplies evidence
for an evaluation criterion and verify that

80
00:07:14.185 --> 00:07:19.599
the proposed scope can be completed for the
assigned credit.

81
00:07:19.599 --> 00:07:27.146
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original proposal.

82
00:07:27.266 --> 00:07:34.146
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

83
00:07:34.146 --> 00:07:34.637
analysis.

84
00:07:34.637 --> 00:07:41.627
They should maintain high standards of
professional competence, conduct, and ethical

85
00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:42.262
practice.

86
00:07:42.262 --> 00:07:49.755
Consider this worked case: The checklist
confirms: proposal drafted, sponsor approved,

87
00:07:49.755 --> 00:07:56.566
deliverables defined, ethics reviewed, final
report ready, and departmental permission

88
00:07:56.566 --> 00:07:57.247
secured.

89
00:07:57.247 --> 00:08:05.331
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

90
00:08:05.331 --> 00:08:06.575
verification step.

91
00:08:06.575 --> 00:08:13.342
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

92
00:08:13.342 --> 00:08:13.825
intuition.
